export const O6={title:'Occupational therapy in mental health',hook:'Occupational therapy began in mental health: in the 1800s, meaningful activity and routine were part of humane treatment, and Eleanor Clarke Slagle’s habit training built daily structure for people in psychiatric hospitals. Today OT helps people with mental illness build routines, skills and roles that support recovery.',objectives:['Describe common mental health conditions and their effects on occupational performance.','Explain the recovery model and OT interventions in mental health.','Plan and lead therapeutic groups.','Apply sensory and coping strategies safely.'],sections:[
['Mental health conditions and occupation',[
'Mental health conditions affect how people think, feel and act—and therefore how they perform daily occupations. **Depression** reduces motivation, energy and concentration, so self-care, work and social participation decline. **Anxiety disorders** cause excessive worry and avoidance. **Bipolar disorder** includes depressive and manic episodes that disrupt routines and judgment. **Schizophrenia** involves psychosis (hallucinations and delusions) and “negative” symptoms such as low motivation and social withdrawal. **Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)** follows trauma with intrusive memories, avoidance and hyperarousal. **Substance use disorders** disrupt roles and health. **Eating disorders**, **obsessive-compulsive disorder** and **neurocognitive disorders** also bring OT referrals. OT focuses on what clients need and want to do—managing a home, finding work, maintaining relationships and caring for health.',
match('Match the condition to a typical effect on occupation',[['Depression','Reduced motivation for self-care and work'],['Anxiety disorder','Avoidance of feared activities'],['Schizophrenia (negative symptoms)','Social withdrawal and low initiative'],['Bipolar disorder (mania)','Impulsive spending and disrupted sleep'],['PTSD','Avoiding places that trigger memories'],['Substance use disorder','Loss of work and family roles']])]],
['The recovery model and OT intervention',[
'The **recovery model** emphasizes hope, self-direction, strengths and living a meaningful life with or without symptoms. OT interventions include **life skills training** (money management, cooking, medication routines, use of transportation), **routine and habit building** (sleep hygiene, structured days), **social skills training**, **vocational** and **supported employment**, **leisure exploration**, **stress management and coping skills**, **sensory modulation** and **psychoeducation**. Approaches draw on the **cognitive-behavioral** frame (changing unhelpful thoughts and behaviors), **psychosocial rehabilitation** and **MOHO** (volition, habituation and performance capacity). OT practitioners work in inpatient psychiatric units, community mental health centers, clubhouses, schools, prisons and homeless services.',
sort('Life skill or coping skill?',['Life skills training','Coping and stress management'],[['Budgeting a weekly allowance',0],['Planning and cooking a meal',0],['Deep breathing during anxiety',1],['Using a bus schedule',0],['Grounding techniques for panic',1],['Journaling feelings',1]])]],
['Therapeutic groups',[
'Groups are a core OT method in mental health because they provide social practice, peer support and efficiency. Group types include **activity groups**, **psychoeducational groups**, **social skills groups**, **task groups** and **support groups**. Mary Cole’s **seven steps** for leading a group are: **introduction** (warm-up and purpose), **activity**, **sharing**, **processing** (feelings about the experience), **generalizing** (lessons learned), **application** (how lessons apply to daily life) and **summary**. Leaders adapt groups to members’ cognitive and emotional levels, set clear rules, ensure safety and encourage participation without forcing it.',
"Groups are also graded to members’ abilities. **Parallel** groups let members work side by side on individual tasks with little interaction—useful for people who are acutely ill or anxious. **Project** and **cooperative** groups require members to share tasks, make decisions and resolve conflicts, building social skills for work and community life. A leader might begin a new member in a parallel crafts group and later invite them into a cooperative meal-planning group as confidence grows. Clear expectations, structure and positive feedback make groups feel safe.",
order('Arrange Cole’s seven steps of group leadership',['Introduction','Activity','Sharing','Processing','Generalizing','Application','Summary'])]],
['Sensory strategies and safety',[
'Many people with mental illness, trauma or developmental differences benefit from **sensory modulation**—using sensory input to manage arousal and emotion. **Sensory rooms** or kits may include weighted blankets, music, textured objects, cold or warm items and calming lighting. Clients learn which inputs calm or alert them. **Safety** is essential: OT practitioners assess suicide and self-harm risk according to setting policy, remove or supervise sharp tools during activities, monitor for agitation, use de-escalation (calm voice, space, choices) and report concerns immediately. Medication side effects—sedation, tremor, weight gain—can also affect occupational performance and should be noted.',
chk('During a cooking group, a member with depression says, “It doesn’t matter what happens to me anymore.” What should the OT practitioner do?',['Take the statement seriously, stay with the client and report it immediately according to policy','Ignore it and continue the recipe','Tell the client to cheer up','Discuss it later in the week'],'Any statement suggesting suicide risk requires immediate action.')]],
['Wellness and prevention in mental health',[
'Wellness includes physical, emotional, social, occupational and spiritual dimensions. **Lifestyle Redesign**, developed at the University of Southern California, helps people build healthy routines through occupation; the Well Elderly Studies showed benefits for older adults. OT promotes **occupational balance**—a healthy mix of self-care, productivity, rest and leisure—and **sleep hygiene**, physical activity and social connection. Early intervention, stress management in schools and workplaces and peer support reduce the impact of mental illness.',
flip('Wellness strategies',[['Occupational balance','A healthy mix of work, rest, self-care and leisure'],['Sleep hygiene','Consistent schedule; limit screens before bed'],['Lifestyle Redesign','Building healthy routines through occupation'],['Peer support','Recovery support from people with lived experience'],['Supported employment','Job placement with ongoing support'],['Mindfulness','Present-moment awareness to reduce stress']])]],
['Applying OT in mental health',[
kase('Building a routine',{a:{text:'A young adult with schizophrenia has stopped bathing and sleeps until afternoon. His goal is to get a part-time job. Where should OT begin?',choices:[['Build a daily routine with small steps—waking time, hygiene and a structured activity','b','Correct. Habits and routines support recovery and work readiness.',true],['Start a full-time job immediately','b','Too large a step; grade toward work.',false]]},b:{text:'He agrees to a morning routine with a checklist. What is a good next step toward work?',choices:[['Volunteer a few hours a week, then move to supported employment','c','Correct. Grading participation builds skills and confidence.',true],['Wait until all symptoms disappear','c','The recovery model supports participation alongside symptoms.',false]]},c:{text:'After three months he follows his routine most days and begins a supported employment program.',end:true}})]],
["Trauma-informed care and substance use",["**Trauma-informed care** recognizes that many clients have experienced trauma and builds safety, trustworthiness, choice, collaboration and empowerment into every interaction. OT practitioners explain each step, ask permission before touch, offer choices, avoid unnecessary restraint and notice triggers such as loud environments or particular positions. Sensory strategies and predictable routines help clients feel safe enough to engage in occupations.","For **substance use disorders**, OT helps people rebuild the routines, roles and leisure that substance use replaced—structuring time, developing healthy leisure, managing stress and cravings with coping skills, and preparing for work and independent living. OT collaborates with counselors, peer recovery specialists and medical providers and supports **harm reduction** and relapse prevention plans. Recovery is often non-linear, so practitioners respond to setbacks without judgment and focus on strengths.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Trauma-informed or not?","buckets":["Trauma-informed","Not trauma-informed"],"items":[["Asking permission before touching a client",0],["Offering a choice of activities",0],["Explaining each step before starting",0],["Insisting the client sit with their back to the door",1],["Using a calm, predictable routine",0],["Raising your voice to gain compliance",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Mental health conditions affect motivation, routines, social participation and roles; OT targets daily occupations and recovery.','Interventions: life skills, routines, social and vocational skills, coping and sensory strategies, psychoeducation; Cole’s seven steps guide groups.','Safety first: take suicide statements seriously and report immediately; promote occupational balance and Lifestyle Redesign.'],terms:[['Recovery model','Approach emphasizing hope, strengths and a meaningful life.'],['Sensory modulation','Using sensory input to regulate arousal and emotion.'],['Occupational balance','A healthy mix of daily activities.']],mcqs:[q('Which OT approach emphasizes hope, self-direction and living a meaningful life?','The recovery model','The biomechanical frame','The developmental frame','Sensory integration only','It is used in mental health practice.'),q('The first step in Cole’s seven steps is:','Introduction','Processing','Summary','Application','Warm-up and purpose.'),q('Negative symptoms of schizophrenia include:','Low motivation and social withdrawal','Hallucinations','Delusions','Mania','Positive symptoms include hallucinations.'),q('Which is a life skill taught in OT mental health groups?','Budgeting money','Interpreting X-rays','Prescribing medication','Suturing','Life skills support independent living.'),q('A client says something suggesting suicide risk. The OT practitioner should:','Take it seriously and report it immediately','Ignore it','Laugh it off','Wait a week','Safety is the priority.'),q('Occupational therapy’s roots include:','Moral treatment in the 1800s','Space exploration','The invention of X-rays','Dental hygiene','Meaningful activity was used humanely.'),q('Lifestyle Redesign focuses on:','Building healthy routines through occupation','Surgery','Medication dosing','Diagnostic imaging','It was developed at USC.'),q('In Cole’s model, “generalizing” means:','Identifying lessons learned from the activity','Starting the activity','Ending the session','Choosing members','It precedes application.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how an occupational therapy practitioner would help an adult with depression return to work and self-care, including at least one group intervention and safety considerations.',rubric:[['Effects of depression on occupation',['motivation','energy','self-care','routine','work','withdraw']],['Individual interventions',['routine','habit','graded','life skill','sleep','coping']],['Group intervention',['group','introduction','sharing','processing','generaliz','application']],['Safety and recovery',['suicide','report','recovery','hope','strength','supported employment']]],model:'Depression reduces energy, motivation and concentration, so the client may neglect bathing, cooking and sleep routines and withdraw from work and friends. Using the recovery model and MOHO, I would build an occupational profile around her values and goals, then help her establish a daily routine with small, achievable steps—a consistent wake time, morning hygiene, a short walk and one meaningful task—grading demands up as her energy improves, along with sleep hygiene and coping strategies such as activity scheduling and breathing techniques. I would invite her to a psychoeducational and activity group, led with Cole’s seven steps: an introduction, a cooking or planning activity, sharing, processing feelings, generalizing lessons, applying them to her own week and a summary. To return to work, I would grade from volunteering or part-time hours to her job, possibly with supported employment and workplace accommodations. Throughout, I would monitor safety: any statement suggesting suicide or self-harm would be taken seriously and reported immediately according to policy.'},sources:[RULES,NIMH,SAMHSA,AOTA]};

export const O7={title:'Occupational therapy for children and youth',hook:'A child’s occupations are play, learning, self-care and friendship. When a disability or delay gets in the way—trouble holding a pencil, overwhelming sensations at recess or difficulty feeding—pediatric OT helps the child participate at home, in school and in the community.',objectives:['Describe typical development and pediatric OT settings, including early intervention and school-based practice.','Explain common pediatric conditions and their effects on occupation.','Apply sensory integration and fine motor strategies.','Describe education laws that guide school-based OT.'],sections:[
['Development and play',[
'Pediatric OT practitioners know **developmental milestones** in gross motor, fine motor, self-care, cognitive, language and social-emotional areas—for example, a **pincer grasp** around 9–12 months, scribbling around 15–18 months, copying a circle around age 3, a cross around age 4 and a triangle around age 5, and dressing with help in the preschool years. **Play** is the primary occupation of childhood and both a goal and a tool of intervention. Grasp patterns progress from **palmar** grasps to a mature **dynamic tripod grasp** for writing. OT considers family routines, culture and environment, and partners with parents in **family-centered** care.',
sort('Earlier or later milestone?',['Earlier (infant or toddler)','Later (preschool or school age)'],[['Pincer grasp',0],['Scribbling with a crayon',0],['Copying a triangle',1],['Tying shoelaces',1],['Transferring a toy hand to hand',0],['Using a dynamic tripod pencil grasp',1]])]],
['Pediatric settings and laws',[
'**Early intervention** serves infants and toddlers from birth to age 3 under **IDEA Part C**, usually in natural environments such as the home, guided by an **Individualized Family Service Plan (IFSP)** that focuses on family priorities. **School-based OT** serves students ages 3–21 under **IDEA Part B**, guided by an **Individualized Education Program (IEP)**; OT is a related service that helps the student benefit from education—handwriting, keyboarding, classroom participation, self-care at school and access to the playground. Students who do not qualify under IDEA may receive accommodations under **Section 504** of the Rehabilitation Act. Hospitals, outpatient clinics and neonatal intensive care units are other pediatric settings.',
"In schools, OT practitioners provide services in several ways: **direct** service with the student (individually or in small groups), **integrated** service within the classroom routine, and **consultation** with teachers and families to adapt materials, schedules and environments. Services are tied to **educational relevance**—for example, a goal about managing lunch containers supports independence in the cafeteria, and a goal about keyboarding supports written assignments. Data are collected regularly to show progress toward IEP goals.",
match('Match the term to its description',[['IDEA Part C','Early intervention from birth to age 3'],['IFSP','Family-focused plan in early intervention'],['IDEA Part B','Special education for ages 3–21'],['IEP','Education plan with goals and related services'],['Section 504','Accommodations for students with disabilities'],['NICU OT','Feeding, positioning and development for newborns']])]],
['Common pediatric conditions',[
'**Cerebral palsy** is a group of movement disorders from early brain injury; OT addresses positioning, hand function, adaptive equipment and ADLs. **Autism spectrum disorder** involves differences in social communication and restricted or repetitive behaviors, often with sensory processing differences. **Developmental coordination disorder** makes motor tasks such as handwriting and sports difficult. **ADHD** affects attention and organization. **Down syndrome** brings low muscle tone and developmental delays. **Duchenne muscular dystrophy**, a progressive X-linked condition mainly affecting boys, causes increasing weakness; in middle school, a student may benefit from **extra time to transition between classes** to conserve energy and stay safe. **Spina bifida**, **juvenile arthritis**, **brachial plexus injury** and **feeding disorders** are other examples.',
match('Match the condition to an OT strategy',[['Cerebral palsy','Positioning and adaptive equipment'],['Autism spectrum disorder','Sensory strategies and visual schedules'],['Developmental coordination disorder','Motor planning practice for handwriting'],['Duchenne muscular dystrophy','Extra time between classes to conserve energy'],['ADHD','Organization strategies and movement breaks'],['Down syndrome','Building fine motor strength and self-care routines']])]],
['Sensory integration and processing',[
'**A. Jean Ayres** developed **sensory integration** theory to explain how the brain organizes sensory input from the body and environment to produce adaptive responses. Children may be **over-responsive** (bothered by noise, tags or touch), **under-responsive** (not noticing input) or **sensory seeking**. Systems include the **tactile**, **vestibular** (movement and balance), **proprioceptive** (body position from muscles and joints), visual, auditory, taste and smell senses. **Ayres Sensory Integration** therapy uses play-based, child-directed activities with suspended equipment in a clinic; classroom strategies include a **sensory diet** (planned sensory activities across the day), movement breaks, fidgets, noise-reducing headphones and weighted items when appropriate. Practitioners avoid overstating claims and track functional outcomes.',
flip('Sensory systems',[['Vestibular','Movement and balance from the inner ear'],['Proprioceptive','Body position from muscles and joints'],['Tactile','Touch, pressure, texture and temperature'],['Over-responsive','Bothered by ordinary sensations'],['Sensory seeking','Craves intense movement or touch'],['Sensory diet','Planned sensory activities through the day']])]],
['Fine motor, handwriting and self-care',[
'Many school referrals concern **handwriting**. OT examines posture and seating, grasp, letter formation, spacing, visual-motor integration, motor planning and endurance, then uses strategies such as pencil grips, slant boards, multisensory letter practice, raised-line paper and keyboarding. **Self-care** goals include feeding, dressing (zippers, buttons, shoes), toileting and hygiene, taught with backward chaining (the child completes the last step first) and visual supports. Interventions are embedded in meaningful play and routines and coordinated with teachers and families.',
kase('A handwriting referral',{a:{text:'A second grader’s handwriting is hard to read, and he tires quickly. During observation, his feet dangle from a too-tall chair. What should the OT address first?',choices:[['Seating and posture—feet flat and desk at the right height','b','Correct. A stable base supports fine motor control.',true],['Have him write the alphabet 100 times','b','Repetition without addressing the cause is ineffective.',false]]},b:{text:'He also uses a fisted grasp. Which strategy fits?',choices:[['Use a pencil grip and play-based fine motor activities to build a tripod grasp','c','Correct. Grade grasp development through play.',true],['Ban drawing until he improves','c','Drawing is useful practice.',false]]},c:{text:'With a footrest, a pencil grip and play-based practice, his legibility and endurance improve over the semester.',end:true}})]],
["Feeding, the NICU and adolescent transition",["**Feeding** is a vital childhood occupation. OT practitioners address oral motor skills, sensory aversions, positioning, self-feeding with adapted utensils and mealtime routines, often with speech-language pathologists. In the **neonatal intensive care unit (NICU)**, OT supports premature and medically fragile infants through developmental positioning, reducing stressful stimuli, supporting feeding readiness, promoting skin-to-skin contact and coaching parents. Signs of infant stress include color changes, finger splaying, gaze aversion and changes in breathing.","For **adolescents**, OT focuses on independence and the **transition** to adulthood: driving or community transportation, money management, cooking, job skills, self-advocacy, social participation and health management. Under IDEA, transition planning must be included in the IEP by the time the student turns 16 (earlier in some states), and OT helps set realistic postsecondary goals for education, employment and independent living.",{"t":"check","q":"By what age must transition planning be included in a student’s IEP under IDEA?","options":["16","10","21","5"],"why":"Some states begin earlier."}]]
],takeaways:['Play is the primary occupation of childhood; know milestones such as pincer grasp, scribbling and copying shapes.','IDEA Part C (birth–3, IFSP), Part B (3–21, IEP; OT as related service), Section 504 accommodations.','Sensory integration (Ayres): vestibular, proprioceptive, tactile; a child with Duchenne muscular dystrophy may need extra time to transition between classes.'],terms:[['IEP','Individualized Education Program for students in special education.'],['Proprioception','Sense of body position from muscles and joints.'],['Backward chaining','Teaching the last step of a task first.']],mcqs:[q('A middle-school child with Duchenne muscular dystrophy may benefit from which support for educational goals?','Additional time to transition between classes','A pencil grip only','A touch-screen board only','A daily quiet study schedule only','A HOSA sample question.'),q('Early intervention for children from birth to age 3 is provided under:','IDEA Part C','IDEA Part B','Section 504','Medicare','Guided by an IFSP.'),q('School-based OT services are described in the student’s:','Individualized Education Program (IEP)','IFSP','Medicare card','Driver’s license','OT is a related service.'),q('Who developed sensory integration theory?','A. Jean Ayres','Gary Kielhofner','Eleanor Slagle','Mary Cole','It is used in pediatrics.'),q('The vestibular system senses:','Movement and balance','Taste','Light','Smell','It is located in the inner ear.'),q('A mature writing grasp is the:','Dynamic tripod grasp','Fisted palmar grasp','Pincer grasp only','Lateral pinch','It develops through the early school years.'),q('The primary occupation of childhood is:','Play','Work','Driving','Managing finances','Play is also a therapeutic tool.'),q('Backward chaining teaches a child to complete:','The last step first','The first step first','All steps at once','No steps','Success builds motivation.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how school-based occupational therapy would support a 7-year-old with autism who is overwhelmed by classroom noise and struggles with handwriting, including the laws and plans that apply.',rubric:[['Laws and plans',['idea','part b','iep','related service','504']],['Sensory strategies',['sensory','over-responsive','headphones','break','sensory diet','quiet']],['Handwriting strategies',['grasp','posture','seating','grip','slant','letter']],['Collaboration and outcomes',['teacher','family','goal','participation','data']]],model:'Under IDEA Part B, the student’s Individualized Education Program can include occupational therapy as a related service to help him benefit from his education; if he did not qualify, Section 504 could provide accommodations. Because he appears over-responsive to sound, I would observe him in class and collaborate with his teacher to create a sensory diet—scheduled movement breaks, a quiet corner, noise-reducing headphones during loud activities and a visual schedule to prepare him for transitions. For handwriting, I would check seating so his feet are flat and the desk is at elbow height, assess grasp, letter formation and visual-motor skills, and use a pencil grip, slant board, raised-line paper and play-based fine motor activities, grading from tracing to copying to independent writing. I would set measurable goals, such as legibly copying a sentence and staying in class during loud activities, collect data, consult regularly with the teacher and family and adjust strategies so he can participate fully in learning and recess.'},sources:[RULES,IDEA,CDC_MILESTONES]};

export const O8={title:'Occupational therapy for adults and older adults',hook:'Which sign suggests that a minor neurocognitive disorder has become major? Not forgetting a new acquaintance’s name—but wearing inappropriate clothing for the weather, a sign that everyday independence is slipping. OT for adults and older adults focuses on keeping people safe, engaged and independent in the places they live.',objectives:['Distinguish minor and major neurocognitive disorders and OT strategies for dementia.','Apply low vision, home modification and driving interventions.','Support aging in place and caregiver training.','Address chronic conditions in adulthood with occupation-based care.'],sections:[
['Normal aging and occupation',[
'Aging brings gradual changes in strength, flexibility, balance, vision, hearing, reaction time and memory retrieval, but most older adults live independently and value meaningful roles—grandparent, volunteer, gardener, friend. OT supports **aging in place**: continuing to live safely at home through adapted routines, environmental changes and community supports. **Productive aging** practice includes fall prevention, driving, low vision, chronic disease self-management, caregiver support and end-of-life care. Occupational deprivation—losing meaningful activity after retirement or relocation—can harm health, so OT helps older adults find new or adapted occupations.',
sort('Typical aging change or something to evaluate further?',['Typical gradual change','Needs further evaluation'],[['Needing reading glasses',0],['Slower reaction time',0],['Getting lost on a familiar route',1],['Taking longer to recall a name',0],['Leaving the stove on repeatedly',1],['Wearing a heavy coat in hot weather',1]])]],
['Neurocognitive disorders and dementia',[
'A **minor (mild) neurocognitive disorder** involves modest cognitive decline that does **not** interfere with independence in everyday activities (the person may need more effort or strategies). A **major neurocognitive disorder** (dementia) involves significant decline that **interferes with independence**—for example, **wearing inappropriate clothing for the weather**, mismanaging money or medications or getting lost. **Alzheimer’s disease** is the most common cause; others include vascular, Lewy body and frontotemporal dementia. OT strategies include establishing consistent **routines**, simplifying tasks into **one-step directions**, using **visual cues** and labels, reducing clutter and noise, matching activities to remaining abilities (the **Allen Cognitive Levels** describe stages), engaging residents in meaningful, failure-free activities, ensuring safety (stove knobs, wandering alarms) and **training caregivers**.',
match('Match the strategy to its purpose',[['Labeling cabinets with pictures','Supports recognition and independence'],['One-step directions','Reduces confusion during tasks'],['Consistent daily routine','Builds habits and reduces agitation'],['Removing stove knobs','Prevents fire risk'],['Caregiver training','Helps family manage care and stress'],['Failure-free activities','Supports engagement and self-esteem']]),
chk('Which behavior may indicate progression from a minor to a major neurocognitive disorder?',['Wearing inappropriate clothing for the weather','Becoming disoriented in a new environment','Withdrawing from a social get-together','Forgetting a new acquaintance’s name the next day'],'Loss of independence in everyday activities marks major neurocognitive disorder.')]],
['Low vision',[
'**Low vision** is vision loss that cannot be fully corrected with glasses, contacts, medicine or surgery. Common causes include **macular degeneration** (central vision loss), **glaucoma** (peripheral loss), **diabetic retinopathy** (patchy loss) and **cataracts** (blurring and glare). OT teaches clients to use remaining vision and other senses through **lighting** (task lighting, reducing glare), **contrast** (dark plate on a light tablecloth, colored tape on stair edges), **magnification**, **enlargement** (large-print and talking devices), **organization** (a place for everything) and **eccentric viewing** to use peripheral vision. These strategies preserve reading, cooking, medication management and safe mobility.',
flip('Low vision strategies',[['Contrast','Dark mug for milk; light mug for coffee'],['Lighting','Bright, even task lighting without glare'],['Magnification','Handheld or electronic magnifiers'],['Enlargement','Large-print labels and phones'],['Organization','Keep items in consistent places'],['Tactile marking','Raised dots on appliance settings']])]],
['Home modification and driving',[
'A **home safety evaluation** identifies hazards and solutions: removing throw rugs and clutter, improving lighting, installing **grab bars**, using a **tub transfer bench** or walk-in shower, a **raised toilet seat**, railings on both sides of stairs, lever door handles and moving frequently used items to waist height. The **Americans with Disabilities Act** sets accessibility standards in public places, and universal design benefits everyone. **Driving** is a key IADL for independence; **driver rehabilitation specialists** (often OTs) evaluate vision, cognition and motor skills, recommend vehicle adaptations such as hand controls and spinner knobs and help clients transition to other transportation when driving is no longer safe.',
"Assistive technology increasingly supports older adults at home: **medication reminder** apps and dispensers, **smart speakers** for reminders and calls, video doorbells, motion-activated lights, **personal emergency response systems** and stove shut-off devices. OT practitioners match technology to the client’s abilities and interests, teach its use step by step and involve family members so that devices actually support daily routines rather than adding confusion.",
sort('Bathroom safety: helpful or hazardous?',['Helpful','Hazardous'],[['Grab bar by the toilet',0],['Tub transfer bench',0],['Using the towel bar for support',1],['Bath mat that slides',1],['Raised toilet seat after hip replacement',0],['Night-light in the hallway',0]])]],
['Chronic conditions and caregivers',[
'Adults live with chronic conditions such as **diabetes**, **heart disease**, **COPD**, **arthritis**, **cancer** and **chronic pain**. OT supports **self-management**: building medication routines, energy conservation, joint protection, pain management strategies, healthy habits and balancing roles. For cancer survivors, OT addresses fatigue, cognitive changes (“chemo brain”) and return to work. **Caregivers**—often spouses or adult children—need training in safe transfers, body mechanics, activity setup and stress management; caregiver strain increases the risk of injury and institutionalization. Respite and support groups help families sustain care.',
kase('Aging in place',{a:{text:'An 82-year-old with macular degeneration and arthritis wants to keep living alone. She has fallen once in the bathroom. What should OT evaluate first?',choices:[['A home safety evaluation focused on the bathroom, lighting and contrast','b','Correct. Her fall and vision loss point to environmental risks.',true],['Her ability to run errands by car','b','Driving may matter later, but the fall site comes first.',false]]},b:{text:'Which modification best fits both her vision and arthritis?',choices:[['Grab bars, a tub transfer bench and bright, glare-free lighting with high-contrast mats','c','Correct. These address balance, joints and vision.',true],['Dim lighting to reduce glare everywhere','c','Too little light increases fall risk.',false]]},c:{text:'With the modifications and large-print, high-contrast labels for her medications, she continues to live independently.',end:true}})]],
["Parkinson’s disease, palliative care and hospice",["**Parkinson’s disease** is common in older adults. Its tremor, rigidity, slowness and shuffling gait affect handwriting (which becomes small—**micrographia**), dressing, eating and mobility. OT strategies include **big, deliberate movements** (amplitude-based training), external **cues** such as counting, rhythm or visual lines on the floor to overcome freezing, weighted utensils and cups with lids, adaptive clothing with magnetic closures, bed rails and transfer poles, and fall prevention. Planning activities when medication is working best (“on” times) helps.","In **palliative care** and **hospice**, OT focuses on comfort, dignity and meaningful participation for people with serious or terminal illness. Interventions include energy conservation, positioning for comfort and skin protection, adaptive equipment that supports self-care as long as possible, caregiver training for safe care, and **legacy activities**—creating memory books, recording stories or writing letters—that help clients find meaning and connect with family. Goals shift from independence to quality of life as the client defines it.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the strategy to its purpose","pairs":[["Big, deliberate movements","Counteract small, slow movements in Parkinson’s"],["Visual lines on the floor","Help overcome freezing of gait"],["Weighted utensils","Reduce the effect of tremor"],["Legacy activities","Create meaning and connection at end of life"],["Positioning for comfort","Prevent pressure injuries in hospice"],["Caregiver training","Support safe care at home"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Minor neurocognitive disorder does not interfere with independence; major does (e.g., wearing inappropriate clothing for the weather).','Dementia strategies: routines, one-step directions, visual cues, failure-free activities, safety and caregiver training.','Low vision: lighting, contrast, magnification, organization; home modifications (grab bars, tub bench, raised toilet seat) and driver rehabilitation support aging in place.'],terms:[['Aging in place','Living safely and independently at home as one ages.'],['Major neurocognitive disorder','Cognitive decline that interferes with daily independence.'],['Low vision','Vision loss not fully correctable with lenses or surgery.']],mcqs:[q('Which behavior may indicate progression from a minor to a major neurocognitive disorder?','Wearing inappropriate clothing for the weather','Forgetting a new acquaintance’s name','Disorientation in a new place','Withdrawing from a party','A HOSA sample question.'),q('Macular degeneration causes loss of:','Central vision','Peripheral vision only','Hearing','Smell','Eccentric viewing can help.'),q('Which strategy uses contrast for low vision?','Placing a dark plate on a light tablecloth','Using dim lighting','Removing labels','Using white-on-white items','Contrast makes objects visible.'),q('A tub transfer bench allows a person to:','Enter the tub while seated','Stand on the tub edge','Skip bathing','Lift heavy objects','It reduces fall risk.'),q('Who evaluates fitness to drive and recommends vehicle adaptations?','A driver rehabilitation specialist','A dental assistant','A pharmacist','A radiologic technologist','Often an OT with specialty training.'),q('Which is a dementia care strategy?','One-step directions and consistent routines','Frequent changes in routine','Long, complex instructions','Arguing to correct confusion','Simplicity reduces agitation.'),q('Aging in place means:','Living safely at home as one ages','Moving to a hospital','Retiring at 50','Changing homes every year','OT supports it with modifications.'),q('Using a towel bar for support in the bathroom is:','Hazardous because towel bars are not designed for weight','Safe','Recommended by OT','Required by law','Install grab bars instead.')],frq:{prompt:'An older adult in early major neurocognitive disorder lives with her daughter. Describe OT strategies to maintain her participation in daily activities and keep her safe, including caregiver training.',rubric:[['Understanding the condition',['major','neurocognitive','dementia','independence','decline']],['Participation strategies',['routine','one-step','visual','cue','failure-free','simplif']],['Safety',['stove','wander','fall','medication','alarm','lighting']],['Caregiver support',['caregiver','training','daughter','respite','stress','transfer']]],model:'Her major neurocognitive disorder means cognitive decline now interferes with everyday independence, so the goal is to maintain participation in meaningful activities as long as possible while keeping her safe. I would establish a consistent daily routine, break tasks such as dressing and setting the table into one-step directions, lay out clothes in order, label cabinets with words and pictures and choose failure-free activities she enjoys, like folding towels or gardening, matched to her current abilities. For safety, I would recommend removing or covering stove knobs, using an automatic shutoff, organizing medications in a locked dispenser managed by her daughter, installing door alarms for wandering, improving lighting, removing rugs and adding grab bars. I would train her daughter in cueing techniques, redirection instead of arguing, safe transfers and body mechanics and in adapting activities as the disease progresses, and connect her with respite care and support groups to manage caregiver stress.'},sources:[RULES,NIA_ALZ,NEI_LV,CDC_STEADI]};

export const O9={title:'Ethics, safety and professional practice in OT',hook:'An OT practitioner sees that a client’s insurance will end before she reaches her goals. Another notices a coworker documenting visits that did not happen. Ethical and safety considerations are 10% of the test—and part of every client encounter, from hand hygiene to honest billing.',objectives:['Apply the principles of the AOTA Code of Ethics.','Describe supervision, documentation and confidentiality requirements.','Apply infection control and safety practices, including transfers and guarding.','Recognize reimbursement and legal issues in practice.'],sections:[
['The AOTA Code of Ethics',[
'The **AOTA Code of Ethics** guides OT practitioners with six principles. **Beneficence**—act for the well-being of clients. **Nonmaleficence**—avoid harm, including keeping skills current and recognizing impairment in oneself or colleagues. **Autonomy**—respect clients’ right to make decisions, obtain informed consent and protect confidentiality. **Justice**—provide fair and equitable services and advocate for access. **Veracity**—be truthful and accurate in communication, documentation and billing. **Fidelity**—treat colleagues and others with respect, fairness and integrity. Ethical dilemmas often pit principles against each other—for example, a client’s autonomy to return home against beneficence when the home is unsafe.',
match('Match the principle to the example',[['Beneficence','Advocating for a wheelchair the client needs'],['Nonmaleficence','Refusing to use a technique you are not trained in'],['Autonomy','Respecting a client’s choice to decline a group'],['Justice','Offering services fairly regardless of insurance'],['Veracity','Documenting exactly what happened in a session'],['Fidelity','Addressing a coworker’s concern respectfully']])]],
['Supervision, scope and documentation',[
'OTs are responsible for evaluation and the overall plan; **OTAs** carry out interventions and contribute to evaluation and documentation **under the supervision of an OT**, as defined by state law and AOTA guidelines. **Aides** perform non-client-related tasks and limited client-related tasks under supervision. Documentation records evaluation, goals, interventions and progress, often in **SOAP** format: **Subjective** (client report), **Objective** (measurable observations), **Assessment** (interpretation) and **Plan**. Goals should be **measurable**—often written in the **ABCD** format (Audience, Behavior, Condition, Degree) or as **SMART** goals. Documentation supports communication, reimbursement and legal protection, and must be accurate, timely and objective.',
sort('Which part of a SOAP note?',['Subjective','Objective','Assessment','Plan'],[['Client states, “My shoulder hurts when I reach.”',0],['Completed upper-body dressing with minimal assistance in 8 minutes',1],['Progressing toward goal; endurance remains limited',2],['Continue three times weekly; add tub transfer training',3]]),
{t:'example',title:'Write a measurable goal',prompt:'Rewrite “Client will get better at dressing” as a measurable goal using the ABCD format.',steps:['Audience: the client.','Behavior: will don a button-up shirt.','Condition: using one-handed technique while seated.','Degree: independently within 5 minutes, in 3 of 4 trials, by the end of 2 weeks.'],answer:'“The client will don a button-up shirt using a one-handed technique while seated, independently within 5 minutes in 3 of 4 trials by the end of 2 weeks.”'}]],
['Confidentiality and consent',[
'OT practitioners protect **protected health information** under **HIPAA** and **FERPA** in schools: discussing clients only with those involved in care, protecting records and screens and obtaining authorization before sharing information. **Informed consent** means clients understand and agree to evaluation and intervention, including risks and alternatives; parents or guardians consent for minors. Clients may refuse treatment, which is documented. Practitioners maintain **professional boundaries**, avoid conflicts of interest and report suspected abuse or neglect as required by law.',
chk('A teacher asks the school OT about a student’s medical diagnosis during lunch in the staff lounge. What is the best response?',['Decline to discuss it there and share only what is appropriate through proper channels','Share all details since the teacher works with the student','Post the information in the staff room','Tell other parents'],'Protect confidentiality and share only what is needed, in private.')]],
['Safety in practice',[
'Safety includes **infection control**—hand hygiene before and after every client (the first and last step of each HOSA skill), cleaning equipment and following standard and transmission-based precautions. **Fall prevention and guarding**: use a **gait belt**, stand on the client’s weaker side, slightly behind, during walking or transfers; lock wheelchair brakes; keep pathways clear; and know the client’s precautions (hip, cardiac, spinal or weight-bearing limits). **Body mechanics** protect the practitioner. Monitor vital signs and symptoms with cardiac and pulmonary clients and stop activity if warning signs appear. Use **sharp tools and heat modalities** carefully, check skin sensation before applying heat or cold, and follow emergency procedures, including CPR certification.',
sort('Safe or unsafe practice?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['Using a gait belt during transfers',0],['Guarding on the client’s weaker side',0],['Leaving wheelchair brakes unlocked during a transfer',1],['Applying heat to skin without checking sensation',1],['Hand hygiene before and after each client',0],['Ignoring a client’s chest pain to finish the activity',1]])]],
['Reimbursement and advocacy',[
'OT services are paid by **Medicare** (Part A for inpatient and skilled nursing, Part B for outpatient), **Medicaid**, private insurance, workers’ compensation and school districts (IDEA). Documentation must show **medical necessity** and skilled services. Ethical practice means billing only for services provided and documenting accurately—fraud harms clients and the profession. OT practitioners also **advocate**: for clients’ access to services and equipment, for policies such as accessible environments and for the profession through AOTA and state associations.',
kase('An ethical dilemma',{a:{text:'A client’s insurance will cover only four more sessions, but she has not met her goal of safe bathing. What is an ethical response?',choices:[['Prioritize the most critical skills, teach a home program and help her explore other resources','b','Correct. Beneficence and justice guide care within real limits.',true],['Document more sessions than she received to get paid','b','That is fraud and violates veracity.',false]]},b:{text:'You notice a coworker billing for sessions that did not happen. What should you do?',choices:[['Report it through the appropriate channels according to policy','c','Correct. Veracity and the duty to protect clients require reporting.',true],['Ignore it to avoid conflict','c','Ignoring fraud allows harm to continue.',false]]},c:{text:'The client completes a home program with a tub bench, and the billing concern is investigated by compliance staff.',end:true}})]],
["Licensure, cultural humility and incident reporting",["Each state regulates OT practice through a **practice act** and licensing board that defines scope, supervision of OTAs, continuing education and discipline. Practitioners practice only within their competence, maintain their licenses and follow both state law and the AOTA Code of Ethics; when they differ, the stricter requirement applies. **Cultural humility**—ongoing self-reflection and respect for clients’ cultures, languages and beliefs—shapes client-centered care, from the foods used in cooking sessions to clothing and modesty preferences during dressing training. Qualified interpreters support accurate communication.","When an **incident** occurs—a fall during treatment, a burn from a heat pack or an equipment failure—the practitioner first ensures the client’s safety and gets help, then notifies the supervisor and completes an **incident (occurrence) report** according to policy. Incident reports support quality improvement and are not part of the client’s medical record. Practitioners also report suspected abuse, neglect or exploitation to the appropriate authorities as required by state law.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Respond to a client fall during treatment","steps":["Stay with the client and do not move them if injury is suspected","Call for help and assess for injury","Notify the supervisor and medical staff","Assist with care as directed","Document objectively and complete an incident report","Review what happened to prevent recurrence"]}]]
],takeaways:['AOTA Code of Ethics: beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, justice, veracity and fidelity.','OTAs work under OT supervision; document in SOAP format with measurable (ABCD or SMART) goals; protect confidentiality under HIPAA and FERPA.','Safety: hand hygiene, gait belt and guarding on the weaker side, client precautions, monitoring cardiac symptoms; bill honestly and advocate for access.'],terms:[['Veracity','Truthfulness in communication and documentation.'],['SOAP note','Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan documentation.'],['Medical necessity','Justification that skilled services are needed.']],mcqs:[q('Which principle requires truthful documentation and billing?','Veracity','Autonomy','Fidelity','Justice','Fraud violates veracity.'),q('Respecting a client’s right to refuse treatment reflects:','Autonomy','Nonmaleficence','Beneficence','Justice','Clients make their own decisions.'),q('Who supervises an OT assistant?','An occupational therapist','A physical therapy aide','A receptionist','No one','Supervision is defined by state law.'),q('In a SOAP note, measurable observations belong under:','Objective','Subjective','Assessment','Plan','Subjective is the client’s report.'),q('When guarding a client during walking, stand:','On the weaker side, slightly behind','In front, facing them','On the stronger side','Several feet away','Use a gait belt.'),q('Before applying heat, the practitioner should:','Check skin sensation','Skip any checks','Apply the hottest setting','Remove the gait belt','Impaired sensation increases burn risk.'),q('ABCD goals include Audience, Behavior, Condition and:','Degree','Diagnosis','Duration only','Doctor','Degree states the measurable criterion.'),q('Which law protects student education records in schools?','FERPA','OSHA','CLIA','DEA','HIPAA protects health information.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe an ethical dilemma an OT practitioner might face, analyze it using at least three principles of the AOTA Code of Ethics, and explain the documentation and safety practices that support ethical care.',rubric:[['Dilemma described',['dilemma','client','insurance','discharge','unsafe','choice']],['Ethical principles applied',['beneficence','nonmaleficence','autonomy','justice','veracity','fidelity']],['Documentation',['soap','measurable','goal','accurate','medical necessity']],['Safety and confidentiality',['hand hygiene','gait belt','guard','hipaa','confidential','precaution']]],model:'A client recovering from a hip fracture insists on returning home alone, but she still needs help with bathing and has fallen twice. Autonomy requires respecting her right to decide after she understands the risks; beneficence asks me to promote her well-being; and nonmaleficence asks me to prevent harm. I would discuss risks honestly (veracity), offer options such as a tub transfer bench, grab bars, a medical alert device, home health services and training for her family, and respect her informed decision while documenting the discussion. Justice means advocating for the services and equipment she needs regardless of insurance limits. Ethical care is supported by accurate, timely SOAP documentation with measurable goals that show medical necessity, billing only for services provided, protecting her information under HIPAA and practicing safely—hand hygiene before and after each session, using a gait belt and guarding on her weaker side, following hip precautions and reporting concerns through proper channels.'},sources:[RULES,AOTA_ETHICS,HIPAA]};

export const O10={title:'Wellness, work and stretch breaks (Skill VI)',hook:'Office workers sit for hours; factory workers repeat the same motions thousands of times; caregivers lift without training. OT promotes health before injury happens—through ergonomics, stretch breaks and healthy routines—and helps injured workers return to their jobs. Skill VI teaches a complete stretch-break routine.',objectives:['Explain OT’s role in health promotion, wellness and occupational balance.','Apply ergonomic principles to prevent work-related musculoskeletal disorders.','Instruct stretch break exercises following HOSA Skill VI.','Describe work rehabilitation and return-to-work services.'],sections:[
['Health promotion and wellness',[
'OT’s **create or promote** approach supports health for everyone, not only people with disabilities. Wellness programs address physical activity, stress management, sleep, nutrition habits, social participation and **occupational balance**. The CDC recommends that adults get at least **150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity** each week plus muscle-strengthening activities on two days, and that people **sit less and move more**. OT practitioners embed activity into daily routines—walking meetings, gardening, active play with children—so healthy habits last. Community programs include fall prevention classes, chronic disease self-management workshops and wellness programs for college students and workers.',
sort('Promotes wellness or increases risk?',['Promotes wellness','Increases risk'],[['Short movement breaks every 30–60 minutes',0],['Sitting for four hours without moving',1],['A consistent sleep schedule',0],['Working through every lunch break',1],['Walking with a friend',0],['Ignoring early wrist pain from typing',1]])]],
['Ergonomics and work-related disorders',[
'**Work-related musculoskeletal disorders** such as low back pain, carpal tunnel syndrome, tendinitis and neck pain result from **risk factors**: **force** (heavy lifting, forceful gripping), **repetition**, **awkward or static postures** (reaching overhead, bending, prolonged sitting), **contact stress**, **vibration** and **cold**. **Ergonomics** fits the job to the worker. At a computer workstation: the top of the monitor at or slightly below eye level about an arm’s length away; the keyboard and mouse at elbow height with straight wrists; feet flat with knees about level with or slightly below the hips; and a chair supporting the lower back. Tool design, job rotation, lift-assist devices and breaks reduce risk. OSHA and NIOSH provide ergonomic guidance.',
match('Match the risk factor to an example',[['Force','Lifting heavy boxes'],['Repetition','Scanning groceries all day'],['Awkward posture','Reaching overhead to stock shelves'],['Static posture','Sitting at a computer for hours'],['Contact stress','Resting wrists on a hard desk edge'],['Vibration','Using power tools for long periods']])]],
['Stretch break exercises (Skill VI)',[
'Brief stretch breaks relieve muscle tension and interrupt static postures. In Skill VI (5 minutes), after hand hygiene, greeting and identifying the patient, the competitor **explains the skill, including how many sets of how many stretches**, then instructs the patient to: **look down and then forward**; **look left and look right**; **tilt the ear to the left shoulder and then to the right shoulder**; **shrug the shoulders**; **pull the shoulders back**; perform a **wrist and elbow stretch by interlacing the hands above the head with elbows extended**; **rotate the hips by lifting the opposite ankle in front of the other knee**; **reach forward toward the toes**; and **lunge forward with a hand on the wall**. Hand hygiene and communication complete the skill (29 points; 70% is 20.3). Stretches should be gentle and pain-free, held without bouncing, with normal breathing.',
order('Stretch break sequence (HOSA Skill VI)',['Explain the number of sets and stretches','Look down, then forward','Look left, then right','Tilt ear to the left shoulder, then the right','Shrug the shoulders','Pull the shoulders back','Interlace hands overhead with elbows extended','Lift the opposite ankle in front of the knee to rotate the hips','Reach forward toward the toes','Lunge forward with a hand on the wall'])]],
['Work rehabilitation and return to work',[
'When workers are injured, OT supports **return to work**. A **functional capacity evaluation (FCE)** measures a person’s physical abilities—lifting, carrying, standing, sitting and endurance—compared with job demands. **Work hardening** is an intensive, job-simulated program that rebuilds strength, endurance and work behaviors; **work conditioning** focuses on physical capacity. A **job site analysis** identifies the physical and cognitive demands of a job and possible accommodations. Under the **Americans with Disabilities Act**, employers provide **reasonable accommodations**—modified schedules, adaptive equipment or job restructuring—for qualified workers with disabilities. OT practitioners also support **transition** from school to work for youth with disabilities.',
match('Match the service to its description',[['Functional capacity evaluation','Measures physical abilities against job demands'],['Work hardening','Job-simulated program to restore work readiness'],['Job site analysis','Identifies demands and accommodations at the workplace'],['Reasonable accommodation','ADA-required change that enables work'],['Ergonomic assessment','Evaluates workstation and task fit'],['Transition planning','Prepares students with disabilities for work']])]],
['Telehealth and emerging practice',[
'OT is expanding into **telehealth** (virtual evaluation and coaching, especially for home modification and caregiver training), **primary care** (addressing habits, routines and chronic disease within medical homes), **community mobility**, **technology and assistive devices** (smart home controls, apps for reminders), **driving and community mobility**, **school mental health** and services for people experiencing **homelessness**. Practitioners must follow state licensure rules for telehealth, protect privacy on secure platforms and adapt assessments for remote delivery.',
kase('An office worker with neck pain',{a:{text:'A data analyst reports neck and shoulder pain by afternoon. Her monitor sits low on a laptop, and she rarely takes breaks. What should OT recommend first?',choices:[['Raise the monitor to eye level with an external keyboard and add regular stretch breaks','b','Correct. Fix the static, awkward posture and interrupt it with breaks.',true],['Work faster to finish earlier','b','Speed does not address posture.',false]]},b:{text:'How would you teach her a stretch break?',choices:[['Explain sets and repetitions, then guide neck, shoulder, wrist, hip and leg stretches gently and without bouncing','c','Correct. This follows the Skill VI sequence.',true],['Have her stretch only when pain is severe','c','Regular breaks prevent pain.',false]]},c:{text:'After two weeks with an adjusted workstation and hourly stretch breaks, her afternoon pain has decreased.',end:true}})]],
["Health management, rest and sleep",["The OTPF includes **health management** as an occupation: managing medications, nutrition, physical activity, symptoms, communication with providers and personal care devices. OT practitioners help clients build medication routines with pill organizers, phone alarms, visual checklists and blister packs; plan meals that fit dietary needs and abilities; schedule exercise into daily life; and use tools to track symptoms such as blood glucose or pain. These strategies are especially important for chronic conditions such as diabetes, heart failure and COPD.","**Rest and sleep** are also occupations. Poor sleep affects mood, attention, pain and safety. OT addresses **sleep preparation** (a consistent bedtime routine, a cool, dark and quiet room, limiting screens and caffeine late in the day) and **sleep participation** (positioning, managing pain or nighttime toileting safely, using night-lights). Balanced routines that include rest breaks during the day—as in energy conservation—help clients sustain participation in meaningful occupations.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Health management or rest and sleep strategy?","buckets":["Health management","Rest and sleep"],"items":[["Pill organizer with phone alarms",0],["Logging blood glucose after meals",0],["Consistent bedtime routine",1],["Limiting screens before bed",1],["Scheduling a daily walk",0],["Night-light for safe bathroom trips",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Promote wellness: CDC recommends 150 minutes of moderate activity weekly, sit less and move more, occupational balance.','Ergonomic risk factors: force, repetition, awkward and static postures, contact stress, vibration; monitor at eye level, keyboard at elbow height, straight wrists.','Skill VI: explain sets; neck (down/forward, left/right, ear to shoulder), shrug, shoulders back, overhead wrist-elbow stretch, hip rotation, reach to toes, wall lunge; return-to-work uses FCEs, work hardening and ADA accommodations.'],terms:[['Ergonomics','Fitting work and tools to the worker.'],['Functional capacity evaluation','Assessment of physical abilities for work.'],['Work hardening','Job-simulated rehabilitation for return to work.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill VI, the competitor first explains:','How many sets of how many stretches','The patient’s diagnosis','The billing code','The patient’s salary','Clear expectations improve participation.'),q('In Skill VI, the wrist and elbow stretch is done by:','Interlacing hands above the head with elbows extended','Making fists behind the back','Pressing palms on the floor','Shaking the hands','A Skill VI step.'),q('The CDC recommends adults get at least how much moderate aerobic activity weekly?','150 minutes','15 minutes','600 minutes','30 minutes','Plus two days of strengthening.'),q('Sitting at a computer for hours without moving is an example of:','Static posture','Vibration','Contact stress','Force','Breaks interrupt static posture.'),q('The top of a computer monitor should be:','At or slightly below eye level','Far above eye level','At waist level','Behind the user','Reduces neck strain.'),q('A functional capacity evaluation measures:','Physical abilities compared with job demands','Blood glucose','Vision only','Personality','It guides return to work.'),q('Under the ADA, employers provide:','Reasonable accommodations','Free cars','Unlimited vacation','Medical diagnoses','For qualified workers with disabilities.'),q('Stretches should be performed:','Gently, without bouncing, and pain-free','As hard as possible','With rapid bouncing','Only when injured','Safety prevents strain.')],frq:{prompt:'Design an occupational therapy wellness program for office workers with neck, shoulder and wrist discomfort, including ergonomic changes and how you would teach the HOSA Skill VI stretch break.',rubric:[['Risk factors and wellness',['static','posture','repetition','break','wellness','activity']],['Ergonomic changes',['monitor','eye level','keyboard','elbow','wrist','chair','feet']],['Skill VI stretches',['sets','look down','left','ear','shrug','shoulders back','interlace','ankle','toes','lunge']],['Program delivery and follow-up',['group','schedule','remind','measure','pain','follow-up']]],model:'Office workers face static postures, repetitive typing and awkward reaching, so the program would combine ergonomics, regular movement and education. I would assess workstations and recommend placing the top of the monitor at or slightly below eye level about an arm’s length away, the keyboard and mouse at elbow height with straight wrists, a chair that supports the lower back and feet flat with knees slightly below the hips, plus a timer or app reminding workers to move every 30–60 minutes. I would teach the stretch break by first explaining the number of sets and stretches, then guiding workers to look down and forward, look left and right, tilt each ear toward the shoulder, shrug, pull the shoulders back, interlace their hands overhead with elbows extended, rotate the hips by lifting one ankle in front of the opposite knee, reach toward the toes and lunge forward with a hand on the wall—gently, without bouncing and with normal breathing. I would add walking meetings and education on sleep and stress, measure discomfort ratings before and after six weeks and adjust the program based on feedback.'},sources:[RULES,OSHA_ERGO,CDC_PA,AOTA]};
